"""Write the bounded physical-review ledger from the lead's adjudicated notes."""
from pathlib import Path
import csv,json
B=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
notes={
'V011':('REFUTED','Several 2-5 ppt broad troughs occur in S63/S87/S94/S96-S98, but their fitted flag phases span the Gaia 21.25591-d orbit and their shapes include sharp discontinuities or gaps. The S94 BTJD 3874.9 trough/reset coincides with V014 and V018 on separate CCDs. S96 has a sudden drop after a data gap and a long recovery; S97 has a step, not a bounded eclipse.','The screened repeat-sector dips do not define same-kind eclipses or a unique photometric clock. The broad S87/S94 depressions alone could include astrophysical variability, so rejection applies to this eclipse selection.','The Gaia orbit may still be real spectroscopically; no target eclipse period, epoch, or depth is defensible.','Seek a compact contact-bearing dip in independent target-centered pixels before reviving an eclipse interpretation.'),
'V012':('REFUTED','Raw S40/S58/S74 windows contain high-frequency oscillations; S59 BTJD 2925 is a multi-day ramp and step. The exact Gaia target is catalogued VSX DSCT|GDOR|SXPHE. Fitted local flagged minima occur at many Gaia 24.43808-d phases.','The 53 detector flags track pulsation extrema and baseline changes, not isolated same-kind eclipses. The Gaia-period diagnostic does not concentrate a contact-bearing dip.','A shallow eclipse beneath the pulsations is not excluded by this bounded review.','Remove the pulsation modes and require a repeat with distinct ingress and egress before pursuing a binary eclipse.'),
'V013':('REFUTED','S28 BTJD 2064.65, 2071.46 and 2078.09 are rounded broad minima about 6.7-6.8 d apart, not the Gaia 35.54514-d spacing. S1/S95/S103/S104 flagged windows show trends, sector discontinuities and no repeat of one compact contact morphology.','The smooth waveform can reflect non-eclipse stellar or orbital variability. No same-kind eclipse clock or source-specific conjunction is established from these SAP events.','A tidal or pulsational interpretation is open; this result rejects the selected eclipse flags, not the Gaia SB2 orbit.','Inspect independent high-resolution target-centered photometry only if a discrete residual dip is found.'),
'V014':('REFUTED','The S94 BTJD 3874.94 candidate is a trough and gap/reset coincident with V011 at 3874.90 and V018 at 3874.82 on separate CCDs. S102 and S104 flags lie on slow ramps and gap-bound steps; the local fits shift far from screening centers in S94. S13 has a sparsely sampled unflagged trough worth preserving as a separate lead, not a demonstrated repeat.','There is positive common-time instrumental evidence for the strongest flag, and the other apparent dips do not form a stable Gaia 42.86678-d eclipse sequence.','The shared event does not exclude unrelated target variability or the sparse S13 trough.','Check the S13 event with target-centered pixels and seek another clean eclipse-like repeat.'),
'V015':('REFUTED','The S19/S25/S52/S57/S73 windows are smooth rounded quasi-sinusoidal waves, frequently with adjacent troughs; S73 BTJD 3308.6 has no distinct contact or flat eclipse core. Numerous 1-3 ppt local fits scatter through the Gaia 33.67324-d phase.','These are persistent physical-looking waves but not established repeat eclipses. A tidal-only or pulsational modulation remains plausible; there is no measured source-specific eclipse conjunction.','Rejecting the screened eclipse interpretation does not refute the spectroscopic SB2.','Model the broad orbital/stellar wave and inspect residuals for a separate reproducible occultation.'),
'V016':('REFUTED','Raw S88 narrow troughs are extrema of an alternating ~0.2037-d waveform. This is half the 0.406024-d VSX EW period of WISE J075144.4-611237, 49.46 arcsec from the queued target; ASAS-SN identifies the same nearby source as TIC 410449414 at 49.22 arcsec. A fresh S88 TESSCut pixel regression at the known half-period peaks at (7,5), by the neighbor WCS (7.38,4.81) and about 2.4 pixels from the target WCS (7.92,7.34). The queued Gaia SB1 period is 51.50601 d.','The near-perfect short clock and independently localized known neighbor defeat assignment of the flags to repeated long-period target eclipses. The short waveform may be a real nearby eclipsing/variable source; pixel attribution is recorded in the helper audit.','The spatial check does not exclude independent target eclipses, so the conclusion is about the screened target eclipse claim.','Use target and neighbor centered differential pixel photometry if an independent long-period dip appears.'),
'V017':('PLAUSIBLE','S50 shows a strong repeatable ~0.28-d waveform with narrow minima and broad maxima, 40-55 ppt in local flagged-window fits. S23 has a much weaker broad depression and a gap; no matching long-period signal is seen in the other sectors. Local minimum spacing has median 0.28472 d; the S50 QLP SAP periodogram peaks at 0.279419 d, both waveform diagnostics rather than an adopted eclipse clock. A TESSCut pixel regression at the QLP period peaks at (9,8), 1.78 pixels from the target WCS (7.22,7.96), while static target flux peaks near (7,8) and a bright column appears at x=9.','The S50 morphology could be a short-period contact binary or a non-eclipse variable in the aperture. The offset suggests contamination but does not securely assign the variable; the independent Gaia cone returned no usable rows and is UNKNOWN rather than a nonmatch. A secure source and eclipse-kind identification are not established, so the screen cannot promote it to PROBABLE or assign the Gaia 30.81626-d SB1 as its clock.','No orbital/eclipse period, epoch or depth is adopted for the queued long-period target. The periodogram and minima spacing are approximate short-waveform diagnostics.','Obtain target-centered difference photometry and test alternating minima and a second sector before assigning an eclipsing source.'),
'V018':('REFUTED','S9/S13/S31/S34/S66 show evolving broad rounded waves, without isolated ingress/egress. S94 BTJD 3874.82 overlaps the V011/V014 common-time trough/step; S96 and other late flags occur near gaps or long ramps. Fitted flagged phases spread across the Gaia 30.58074-d orbit.','The apparent repeat-sector dips do not form a stable same-kind eclipse family. Smooth tidal or pulsational variability remains physically possible, but does not satisfy the eclipse definition.','Small hidden eclipses are not ruled out under the changing baseline.','Model the broad wave and look for a separate repeated compact residual event.'),
'V019':('REFUTED','S18/S24/S58/S78/S85 exhibit a rapid continuous waveform with ~0.47452-d minimum spacing in S85; both 45-125 ppt local fitted trough depths and large brightenings are parts of that waveform. VSX lists the exact Gaia target as ROT, and a brighter source 52.55 arcsec away, V1036 Cas, as TTS/ROT at 0.471 d; ASAS-SN lists that neighbor as variable.','The short smooth rotational-like train is not a series of 46.71564-d Gaia SB1 eclipses. Blending of the bright neighbor is material, so the waveform cannot be assigned wholesale to the queued star.','The existing rotation classifications and blended aperture do not exclude a faint separate eclipse.','Resolve target and V1036 Cas with spatially separated photometry if a long-period residual survives.'),
'V020':('REFUTED','S1/S12/S13/S67/S93 selected minima are successive troughs in an oscillating waveform, with frequent comparable maxima. S93 shows smooth oscillations bracketing the 3849.85 flag. VSX classifies the exact Gaia target as DSCT|GDOR|SXPHE. The 51 flagged local fit phases scatter across the Gaia 30.15502-d orbit.','No contact-bearing, same-kind recurrence or compatible conjunction timing emerges from the screened dips. The exact pulsator classification supports the non-eclipse explanation.','A low-amplitude eclipse hidden beneath pulsations remains untested.','Prewhiten the pulsations and inspect only persistent compact residual dips.'),
}
rows=[]
for q in csv.DictReader((B/'queue.csv').open(newline='',encoding='utf8')):
    sid=q['sample_id'];tic=q['tic']; verdict,obs,meaning,limit,nextcheck=notes[sid]
    report=f'''# {sid} TIC {tic}: {verdict}\n\nSelected stratum: {q['sample_stratum']}. Bounded physical review: COMPLETE. Catalogue novelty: UNKNOWN; the independent catalogue audit is not a new-system assignment.\n\n## Raw observations and alternative explanation\n\n{obs}\n\n## Timing, attribution and geometry\n\n{meaning}\n\n## Limits and next check\n\n{limit} Next: {nextcheck}\n\n## Evidence\n\nOriginal cached scan: `../../../binary-exploration-2026-11-04/bulk-results/{tic}.json`. `screen.json` and `all-sectors.png` cover every cached sector's quality-zero finite positive raw SAP; `ranked-windows.png` shows representative context. `window-audit.json` gives actual core cadence coverage, endpoints, gaps, local side/core medians and FITS source hashes for every flagged dip. `fitted-windows.json` and `fitted-windows.png` are provisional flagged-window trapezoids, not adopted eclipse timings; singular/bound-hitting fits have no precision standing. `fold-gaia.json` and `fold-gaia.png` are diagnostic only and the three-day detrend can attenuate broad events. Where present, `short-clock.json` measures waveform minima, not verified eclipses. S94 common-time comparison is in `../cross-target.json` and `../cross-target-s94.png`. Exact target catalogue receipts are indexed at `../../novelty-audit/live/target-results.json`; catalogued *variable* status is separate from novel *binary* status. Every cached FITS was checked against its original SHA-256 and TIC header. The helper's independent pixel/source audit and its caveats are in `../HELPER-AUDIT.md`. No precision eclipse parameters or new-system claims are made.\n'''
    (B/tic/'REPORT.md').write_text(report,encoding='utf8')
    rows.append({'sample_id':sid,'tic':tic,'sample_stratum':q['sample_stratum'],'physical_verdict':verdict,'full_review_completion':'COMPLETE','novelty_status':'UNKNOWN','period_days':'','period_error_days':'','primary_epoch_btjd':'','depth_ppt':'','limitations':limit,'next_check':nextcheck,'report':f'{tic}/REPORT.md'})
with (B/'RESULTS.csv').open('w',newline='',encoding='utf8') as f:
    w=csv.DictWriter(f,fieldnames=list(rows[0]));w.writeheader();w.writerows(rows)
print('wrote',len(rows),'reports and rows')
